secur32: implement RFC 4178 SPNEGO in the Negotiate package, both roles

Power BI Desktop's report canvas is gated by one call - IDBInitialize::Initialize() on the OLE DB
provider msolap.dll - and under Wine that call failed with 0x80070001 and no OLE DB error
information, which the app reports as:

    DataMashup.Trace Critical: … DataExtensionErrorDetails: Details: ErrorCode=OpenConnectionError,
      ProviderErrorCode=0x00000000, ErrorSource=PowerBI, Message=Failed to open the MSOLAP connection.,
      HResult=0x80070001, … ErrorSourceOrigin=MsolapWrapper …
    PBI.Dashboard.ErrorDialog: errorType=ExplorationContainer_FailedToLoadModel_DefaultDetails,
      activityName=PBI.EX.LoadExplorationVisuals

The provider connects to the Analysis Services engine (msmdsrv.exe) and authenticates the XMLA
<Authenticate>/<SspiHandshake> exchange with SSPI, asking for the package "Negotiate" by name
(AcquireCredentialsHandleW(principal=NULL, package="Negotiate", usage=SECPKG_CRED_OUTBOUND)) and
driving InitializeSecurityContextW itself: it transmits a token only while the status is
SEC_I_CONTINUE_NEEDED and treats SEC_E_OK as "nothing left to send".  That is the documented round
shape of SPNEGO, which is what "Negotiate" is - and it is not the shape Wine had.

Reproduction (the same provider, the same engine, one binary on both platforms):

    # engine (Wine): starts msmdsrv.exe exactly as the app does and publishes the port
    EXTRA='--sspi --hook' tools/msolap/connect_test.sh <tag> '' 15
    # client: tools/winapi/msolapprobe.c, built once, run under Wine and on a Windows guest
    #   LoadLibraryExW(msolap.dll) -> DllGetClassObject(CLSID_MSOLAP) -> CreateInstance(IDBInitialize)
    #   -> SetProperties(DBPROP_INIT_PROVIDERSTRING) -> Initialize()

Before the fix, Wine's per-round trace against a live engine (logs/msolap/sspi11.probe.out):

    [sspi] InitializeSecurityContextW #1 -> status=0x00090312 attr=0x1081c
    [sspi]   out: 40 bytes  [NTLMSSP....]  first=4e54 0100
    [sspi] InitializeSecurityContextW #2 -> status=0x00000000 attr=0x1001e
    [sspi]   out: 88 bytes  [NTLMSSP....]  first=4e54 0300
    IDBInitialize::Initialize()                hr=0x80070001  FAIL

and on Windows, same probe, same engine (logs/msolap/winprobe_engine_ok.out):

    [sspi] InitializeSecurityContextW #1 -> status=0x00090312 attr=0x1000
    [sspi]   out: 121 bytes  [`w..+......]  first=6077 0502
    [sspi] InitializeSecurityContextW #2 -> status=0x00090312 attr=0x1001e
    [sspi]   out: 121 bytes  [.w0u......Z]  first=a177 01a2
    [sspi] InitializeSecurityContextW #3 -> status=0x00000000 attr=0x1001e
    [sspi]   out: 0 bytes (empty)
    IDBInitialize::Initialize()                hr=0x00000000  OK
    MSOLAP probe: SUCCESS

The failing step is the status/token *combination* of the second call: Wine returned SEC_E_OK
*together with* the NTLM authenticate message (type 3), so the provider - which only transmits on
SEC_I_CONTINUE_NEEDED - never sent it, and the engine dropped the socket (one client message in
logs/msolap/sspi12.transcript.txt, no second WSASend in a +relay capture of msolap's own calls).

What Windows does, and what this patch restores, is SPNEGO (RFC 4178): the initial token is an ASN.1
NegTokenInit carrying the SPNEGO OID 1.3.6.1.5.5.2, a mechTypes list and the mechanism's first token;
the acceptor answers with a NegTokenResp; and the exchange has one more round than the mechanism,
ending with an empty token and SEC_E_OK.  Wine's "Negotiate" advertised SECPKG_FLAG_GSS_COMPATIBLE and
RPC_C_AUTHN_GSS_NEGOTIATE (`nego_SpGetInfo`) but did no framing at all: dlls/secur32/negotiate.c is
only the user-mode half (seal/unseal delegation), and the LSA-mode package in
programs/lsass/negotiate.c - bound as package 0 by lsass.c's
`init_package( L"", nego_SpLsaModeInitialize )` - forwarded the sub-package's token and status
verbatim.  This patch makes the advertisement true.

Rationale, and what was *not* needed:

  * Both roles are implemented, because both ends of this connection are Wine's: the client is
    msolap.dll in the app's process, the server is Wine's msv1_0 in lsass driven by the engine
    process's AcceptSecurityContext.  A client-side-only change is measurably insufficient -
    presenting the raw NTLM flow in the SPNEGO round shape (the probe's `--spnego` emulation) gets the
    authenticate message onto the wire but then ends in SEC_E_INVALID_TOKEN, because Wine's acceptor
    sends a 0-byte final token where SPNEGO answers a final NegTokenResp
    (logs/msolap/spg1.probe.out).
  * The ASN.1 is hand-rolled and fixed-shape (NegTokenInit / NegTokenResp, definite lengths only) - a
    general ASN.1 library would be more code than the protocol needs.  It lives in the same file as
    the package it serves, so the change touches exactly one pristine file.
  * `SECPKG_ATTR_PACKAGE_INFO` is left delegating to the sub-package.  Windows returns the *negotiated*
    mechanism there - the provider's own query on a Windows Negotiate context answers
    `name="NTLM" caps=0x2882b37 maxToken=2888` immediately before `Initialize() = S_OK`
    (logs/msolap/winprobe_sspi_ref.txt, line 183) - so the pre-existing delegation was already
    correct.  An earlier revision of this patch answered "Negotiate" instead; it was measured and
    reverted, because it was a divergence from Windows, not a fix.
  * The user-mode mapping is re-published on the extra round.  The user-mode half registers itself under the
    address of the caller-side context object's handle field (lsa.c: `LSA_USER_HANDLE(&handle)`), and secur32
    replaces that object on *every* successful round (`new_context->dwLower = new_lsa_ctx`), while
    `InitUserModeContext` only runs when the package reports the context as mapped.  msv1_0 maps on the round
    it completes - round 2 here - so without help the caller's round-3 object would be unregistered and
    `EncryptMessage`/`MakeSignature` would then fail with `SEC_E_INVALID_HANDLE` from
    `find_user_ctx` in `dlls/secur32/negotiate.c`.  The package therefore keeps a copy of the sub-package's
    user-mode context blob and re-publishes it on any later round the mechanism produces no mapping for.
    Raw NTLM never hit this because its last round *is* the mechanism's completing round; the extra round
    SPNEGO requires is what exposes it.
  * mechListMIC is accepted and ignored but not produced: RFC 4178 makes it optional, it is only
    defined once a session key exists, and Wine's NTLM package exposes no MIC facility to this layer.
    Both ends of this connection are Wine, so no peer requires it.
  * Nothing else had to change: no NTLM change, no msv1_0 change, no lsass dispatch change, and
    rpcrt4/winhttp/credui call sites are untouched.  Sealing/unsealing keeps working because the
    Negotiate context still maps to the sub-package's user-mode context exactly as before.  A peer
    that speaks the sub-package's protocol directly still gets the old verbatim pass-through, so
    nothing that worked before regresses.
  * The context attributes reported on the initial call are masked to ISC_RET_INTERMEDIATE_RETURN,
    which is what Windows returns before a mechanism has been picked; later rounds report the
    sub-package's (Wine's NTLM returns 0x1081c where Windows' SPNEGO reports 0x1001e - the set is the
    mechanism's, not something the wrapper invents).

Verified:

  * Codec unit test (the real functions, extracted verbatim, built for the host): round trips of
    NegTokenInit / NegTokenResp / accept-completed with exact expected bytes, decoding of a
    Windows-shaped 159-byte NegTokenResp, and 400000 malformed/truncated/mutated inputs without a
    crash or a hang.  It found two real defects during development - the acceptor's first call did
    not pass the credential handle to msv1_0, and the decoder did not unwrap the OCTET STRING inside
    `[2]` - both fixed before the captures below.
  * Local handshake, same binary on both platforms (logs/msolap/ntlmprobe_fixed_wine.txt vs
    logs/msolap/ntlmprobe_windows2.txt), package "Negotiate":

        Wine  (fixed): ISC#1 CONTINUE  74 B [`H..+...] attr=0x1000
                       ASC   CONTINUE 307 B
                       ISC#2 CONTINUE 115 B [.q0o....]
                       ASC   OK         9 B [..0.....]
                       ISC#3 OK         0 B
        Windows      : ISC#1 CONTINUE 121 B [`w..+...] attr=0x1000
                       ASC   CONTINUE 159 B
                       ISC#2 CONTINUE 121 B [.w0u....]
                       ASC   OK        29 B [..0.....]
                       ISC#3 OK         0 B

    Every status, the round count and the framing at each offset now match; the token *sizes* cannot,
    because the mechanism's own tokens differ between the platforms (Wine's NTLM sends 40/272/88
    bytes where Windows sends 53/128/94), so only the DER length bytes differ.  Judged by
    tools/msolap/spnego_shape.py: round #1 framing PASS, status sequence PASS, terminal empty token
    PASS, round count PASS, outcome PASS.
  * The explicit "NTLM" package is unchanged against the pre-fix Wine capture
    (spnego_shape.py --expect-ntlm: PASS), `AcquireCredentialsHandleW(NULL, NULL, …)` still fails with
    SEC_E_SECPKG_NOT_FOUND on both platforms, and a client that speaks raw NTLM to the *Negotiate*
    acceptor still gets the old pass-through (logs/msolap/negotiate_server_rawpassthrough.txt: raw
    type 1 / type 2 / type 3, `handshake ended: client=0, server=0`).
  * Engine-free proof of the whole post-handshake surface (`logs/msolap/sealprobe_fixed_wine.txt`; the probe
    has the same shape as `tools/winapi/ntlmprobe.c` - a loopback Negotiate handshake in one process, then
    `QCA(SIZES)`, `QCA(PACKAGE_INFO)`, `FreeContextBuffer` and `EncryptMessage`):

        handshake round 1: client=0x00090312 out=74
        handshake done:      client=0x00000000 server=0x00000000
        QCA(SIZES)           status=0x00000000  cbMaxToken=1904 cbMaxSignature=16 cbBlockSize=0 cbSecurityTrailer=16
        QCA(PACKAGE_INFO)    status=0x00000000  name=NTLM
        FreeContextBuffer(lsa buffer) status=0x00000000  PASS  (want 0x00000000, as Windows returns)
        FreeContextBuffer(heap buffer) status=0x00000000  PASS  (the RtlFreeHeap branch)
        EncryptMessage       status=0x00000000  PASS  (user-mode context registered for this handle)

    and with `WINEDEBUG=+secur32` the mapping is visible rather than inferred - three
    `nego_SpInitUserModeContext` registrations (client round 2, server round 2, and the client's extra round,
    re-published) with the seal on the object registered last, i.e. the one the caller holds:

        0178:trace:secur32:nego_SpInitUserModeContext 7ffffe825ae0, …
        0178:trace:secur32:nego_SpInitUserModeContext 7ffffe825b40, …
        0178:trace:secur32:nego_SpInitUserModeContext 7ffffe825ba0, …
        0178:trace:secur32:nego_SpSealMessage 7ffffe825ba0, 0, …

    Before the re-publish that third registration was absent and `EncryptMessage` returned 0x80090301
    (`SEC_E_INVALID_HANDLE`), measured by the provider slice on a session running an earlier revision of this
    build (`logs/msolap/gA.probe.out`, `gB`, `gC`): the visible protocol was complete and identical to
    Windows, and only the caller-side mapping was missing.
  * Provider differential against a live engine (`--sspi --hook`, engine started by
    tools/msolap/connect_test.sh), judged on the capture by the provider slice - all four shape
    criteria pass:

        round #1 framing       out=74   `60 48 05 02 …`   (byte0 0x60 + OID 1.3.6.1.5.5.2)
        rounds 2..n-1 framing  out=115  `a1 71 30 6f a0 03 0a 01 01 …`   (byte0 0xa1)
        status sequence        `00090312 00090312 00000000`, terminal token empty
        round count            3   (Windows reference: 3)

    The wire shows the consequence: the provider sends the 440 B `application/sx+xpress` message
    carrying the NTLMSSP authenticate (it used to drop it), the engine answers with the 9-byte
    `a1 07 30 05 a0 03 0a 01 00`, and the provider consumes it with `ISC#3 -> SEC_E_OK`.
  * End-to-end outcome: with this patch alone `IDBInitialize::Initialize()` still returned 0x80070001 (measured
    twice, `logs/msolap/gate3.probe.out` / `gate4.probe.out`), i.e. the framing removed the failure this patch
    is about - the dropped authenticate message is on the wire - and left two further defects on the same path
    that measuring exposed in turn: the `FreeContextBuffer` return (`0004-secur32-LsaFreeReturnBuffer.patch`)
    and the user-mode mapping re-published here.  With all of it installed the provider differential passes all
    five shape criteria and **`IDBInitialize::Initialize()` returns 0x00000000**
    (`logs/msolap/gate13.run.txt`), which is the acceptance for the task; the application-level canvas
    acceptance runs on the same build and is recorded in `FINDINGS.md` (M41) and
    `docs/MSOLAP_CONNECTION.md` (§8).

Not covered by this patch, stated plainly:

  * mechListMIC is neither produced nor required (see above), so this is not a complete RFC 4178
    implementation - it is the NegTokenInit/NegTokenResp exchange that "Negotiate" needs to be usable,
    including the malformed-input handling (SEC_E_INVALID_TOKEN, no crash, no loop) and the raw
    pass-through for peers that do not speak SPNEGO.
  * The mechanism list offered is a single mechanism (NTLM, or Kerberos when Wine's Kerberos package
    produces a token).  Windows offers a list and can be negotiated down; Wine's Kerberos does not
    complete in this configuration, so the list carries whichever mechanism actually produced the
    token.
  * The acceptor's terminal token is a 9-byte NegTokenResp with negState accept-completed and no MIC;
    Windows' is 29 bytes because it carries a 16-byte mechListMIC.  The initiator only needs a
    non-empty final response before returning SEC_E_OK.
--- a/programs/lsass/negotiate.c
+++ b/programs/lsass/negotiate.c
@@ -34,6 +34,14 @@
     SECPKG_FUNCTION_TABLE      *ntlm;
     LSA_SEC_HANDLE              handle_krb;
     LSA_SEC_HANDLE              handle_ntlm;
+    ULONG                       state;      /* enum nego_state: how far the SPNEGO exchange has got */
+    ULONG                       mode;       /* enum nego_mode: SPNEGO framing, or raw pass-through */
+    ULONG                       attr;       /* context attributes the sub-package negotiated */
+    const BYTE                 *oid;        /* DER OID of the mechanism in use */
+    ULONG                       oid_len;
+    BYTE                       *user_data;  /* copy of the sub-package's user-mode context data */
+    ULONG                       user_data_len;
+    ULONG                       user_ssp;   /* struct user_context_data.ssp for that copy */
 };
 
 struct user_context_data
@@ -68,6 +76,499 @@
     SECPKG_FLAG_LOGON             | \
     SECPKG_FLAG_RESTRICTED_TOKENS )
 
+/* RFC 4178 SPNEGO.
+ *
+ * "Negotiate" is the GSS-API SPNEGO mechanism: its tokens carry an ASN.1 layer around the token of the
+ * mechanism that is actually being negotiated (Kerberos or NTLM here), and the exchange has one more round
+ * than the mechanism itself.  Wine used to forward the sub-package's token and status verbatim, which is
+ * wrong on the wire - the second client call returned SEC_E_OK *together with* the NTLM authenticate message,
+ * and a caller that transmits a token only while the status is SEC_I_CONTINUE_NEEDED, which is what Power BI's
+ * MSOLAP OLE DB provider does, then dropped that message, so the Analysis Services session was never
+ * authenticated and IDBInitialize::Initialize() failed with 0x80070001.
+ * docs/MSOLAP_CONNECTION.md has the measurement; logs/msolap/winprobe_engine_ok.out the Windows shape.
+ */
+
+enum nego_state
+{
+    NEGO_STATE_INIT,         /* client: nothing produced yet; server: nothing consumed yet */
+    NEGO_STATE_NEGOTIATING,  /* a sub-package token was exchanged and the sub-package is not finished */
+    NEGO_STATE_AWAIT_FINAL,  /* client: the sub-package finished and its last token was sent */
+    NEGO_STATE_DONE          /* the exchange is complete */
+};
+
+enum nego_mode
+{
+    NEGO_MODE_UNDECIDED,     /* no peer token seen yet: the initial token is always framed */
+    NEGO_MODE_SPNEGO,        /* the peer speaks SPNEGO */
+    NEGO_MODE_RAW            /* the peer speaks the sub-package's protocol directly */
+};
+
+#define SPNEGO_NEG_ACCEPT_COMPLETED  0
+#define SPNEGO_NEG_ACCEPT_INCOMPLETE 1
+#define SPNEGO_NEG_REJECT            2
+
+static const BYTE oid_spnego[] = {0x2b, 0x06, 0x01, 0x05, 0x05, 0x02};                         /* 1.3.6.1.5.5.2 */
+static const BYTE oid_krb5[]   = {0x2a, 0x86, 0x48, 0x86, 0xf7, 0x12, 0x01, 0x02, 0x02};       /* 1.2.840.48018.1.2.2 */
+static const BYTE oid_ntlm[]   = {0x2b, 0x06, 0x01, 0x04, 0x01, 0x82, 0x37, 0x02, 0x02, 0x0a}; /* 1.3.6.1.4.1.311.2.2.10 */
+static const BYTE ntlmssp_sig[] = {'N', 'T', 'L', 'M', 'S', 'S', 'P', 0};
+
+struct spnego_msg
+{
+    enum { SPNEGO_MSG_NONE, SPNEGO_MSG_INIT, SPNEGO_MSG_RESP } kind;
+    int neg_state;          /* -1 when absent, else one of SPNEGO_NEG_* */
+    const BYTE *token;      /* mechToken / responseToken, pointing into the token being decoded */
+    ULONG token_len;
+    BOOL mech_types_ntlm;
+    BOOL mech_types_krb5;
+};
+
+static BOOL asn1_read_len( const BYTE **p, const BYTE *end, ULONG *len )
+{
+    ULONG n, v = 0;
+
+    if (*p >= end) return FALSE;
+    if (**p < 0x80)
+    {
+        *len = **p;
+        (*p)++;
+        return TRUE;
+    }
+    n = **p & 0x7f;
+    (*p)++;
+    if (!n || n > sizeof(v) || (ULONG)(end - *p) < n) return FALSE; /* indefinite lengths are not DER */
+    while (n--) v = (v << 8) | *(*p)++;
+    *len = v;
+    return TRUE;
+}
+
+static BOOL asn1_read_tlv( const BYTE **p, const BYTE *end, BYTE tag, const BYTE **content, ULONG *len )
+{
+    if (end - *p < 2 || **p != tag) return FALSE;
+    (*p)++;
+    if (!asn1_read_len( p, end, len )) return FALSE;
+    if (*len > (ULONG)(end - *p)) return FALSE;
+    *content = *p;
+    *p += *len;
+    return TRUE;
+}
+
+static BOOL oid_is( const BYTE *oid, ULONG len, const BYTE *want, ULONG want_len )
+{
+    return len == want_len && !memcmp( oid, want, len );
+}
+
+static BOOL looks_like_ntlm( const BYTE *token, ULONG len )
+{
+    return token && len >= sizeof(ntlmssp_sig) && !memcmp( token, ntlmssp_sig, sizeof(ntlmssp_sig) );
+}
+
+static void parse_mech_types( const BYTE *p, const BYTE *end, struct spnego_msg *msg )
+{
+    const BYTE *content, *oid;
+    ULONG len, oid_len;
+
+    if (asn1_read_tlv( &p, end, 0x30, &content, &len ))
+    {
+        p = content;
+        end = content + len;
+    }
+    while (end - p >= 2)
+    {
+        if (!asn1_read_tlv( &p, end, 0x06, &oid, &oid_len )) break;
+        if (oid_is( oid, oid_len, oid_ntlm, sizeof(oid_ntlm) )) msg->mech_types_ntlm = TRUE;
+        else if (oid_is( oid, oid_len, oid_krb5, sizeof(oid_krb5) )) msg->mech_types_krb5 = TRUE;
+    }
+}
+
+static void parse_token_field( const BYTE *content, ULONG len, const BYTE **token, ULONG *token_len )
+{
+    const BYTE *q = content, *octet;
+    ULONG octet_len;
+
+    /* [2] wraps an OCTET STRING; a peer that encodes the value implicitly is tolerated too */
+    if (asn1_read_tlv( &q, content + len, 0x04, &octet, &octet_len ))
+    {
+        *token = octet;
+        *token_len = octet_len;
+    }
+    else
+    {
+        *token = content;
+        *token_len = len;
+    }
+}
+
+static void parse_init_fields( const BYTE *p, const BYTE *end, struct spnego_msg *msg )
+{
+    const BYTE *content;
+    ULONG len;
+
+    msg->kind = SPNEGO_MSG_INIT;
+    if (end - p >= 2 && *p == 0x30 && asn1_read_tlv( &p, end, 0x30, &content, &len ))
+    {
+        p = content;
+        end = content + len;
+    }
+    while (end - p >= 2)
+    {
+        BYTE tag = *p;
+
+        if (!asn1_read_tlv( &p, end, tag, &content, &len )) break;
+        switch (tag & 0x1f)
+        {
+        case 0:  parse_mech_types( content, content + len, msg ); break; /* mechTypes */
+        case 2:  parse_token_field( content, len, &msg->token, &msg->token_len ); break;
+        default: break;                                                /* mechListMIC: ignored */
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+static void parse_resp_fields( const BYTE *p, const BYTE *end, struct spnego_msg *msg )
+{
+    const BYTE *content;
+    ULONG len;
+
+    msg->kind = SPNEGO_MSG_RESP;
+    if (end - p >= 2 && *p == 0x30 && asn1_read_tlv( &p, end, 0x30, &content, &len ))
+    {
+        p = content;
+        end = content + len;
+    }
+    while (end - p >= 2)
+    {
+        BYTE tag = *p;
+
+        if (!asn1_read_tlv( &p, end, tag, &content, &len )) break;
+        switch (tag & 0x1f)
+        {
+        case 0:  if (len >= 3 && content[0] == 0x0a && content[1] == 0x01) msg->neg_state = content[2]; break;
+        case 2:  parse_token_field( content, len, &msg->token, &msg->token_len ); break;
+        default: break;                                                /* supportedMech, mechListMIC */
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+static NTSTATUS spnego_decode( const BYTE *data, ULONG len, struct spnego_msg *msg )
+{
+    const BYTE *p = data, *end = data + len, *content, *oid;
+    ULONG clen, oid_len;
+
+    memset( msg, 0, sizeof(*msg) );
+    msg->neg_state = -1;
+    if (!len) return SEC_E_INVALID_TOKEN;
+
+    if (*p == 0x60)     /* InitialContextToken [APPLICATION 0] { MechType, innerContextToken } */
+    {
+        if (!asn1_read_tlv( &p, end, 0x60, &content, &clen )) return SEC_E_INVALID_TOKEN;
+        p = content;
+        end = content + clen;
+        if (!asn1_read_tlv( &p, end, 0x06, &oid, &oid_len )) return SEC_E_INVALID_TOKEN;
+        if (!oid_is( oid, oid_len, oid_spnego, sizeof(oid_spnego) )) return SEC_E_INVALID_TOKEN;
+        if (end - p < 2) return SEC_E_INVALID_TOKEN;
+    }
+    if (*p == 0xa0)     /* negTokenInit */
+    {
+        if (!asn1_read_tlv( &p, end, 0xa0, &content, &clen )) return SEC_E_INVALID_TOKEN;
+        parse_init_fields( content, content + clen, msg );
+        return SEC_E_OK;
+    }
+    if (*p == 0xa1)     /* negTokenResp */
+    {
+        if (!asn1_read_tlv( &p, end, 0xa1, &content, &clen )) return SEC_E_INVALID_TOKEN;
+        parse_resp_fields( content, content + clen, msg );
+        return SEC_E_OK;
+    }
+    if (*p == 0x30)     /* NegTokenResp without the [1] wrapper */
+    {
+        if (!asn1_read_tlv( &p, end, 0x30, &content, &clen )) return SEC_E_INVALID_TOKEN;
+        parse_resp_fields( content, content + clen, msg );
+        return SEC_E_OK;
+    }
+    return SEC_E_INVALID_TOKEN;
+}
+
+static ULONG der_len_size( ULONG len )
+{
+    if (len < 0x80) return 1;
+    if (len <= 0xff) return 2;
+    if (len <= 0xffff) return 3;
+    return 4;
+}
+
+static ULONG der_tlv_size( ULONG content )
+{
+    return 1 + der_len_size( content ) + content;
+}
+
+static BYTE *der_put_len( BYTE *p, ULONG len )
+{
+    if (len < 0x80) *p++ = len;
+    else if (len <= 0xff) { *p++ = 0x81; *p++ = len; }
+    else if (len <= 0xffff) { *p++ = 0x82; *p++ = len >> 8; *p++ = len; }
+    else { *p++ = 0x83; *p++ = len >> 16; *p++ = len >> 8; *p++ = len; }
+    return p;
+}
+
+static BYTE *der_put_tlv( BYTE *p, BYTE tag, ULONG content )
+{
+    *p++ = tag;
+    return der_put_len( p, content );
+}
+
+static BYTE *der_put_octets( BYTE *p, BYTE tag, const BYTE *data, ULONG len )
+{
+    p = der_put_tlv( p, tag, len );
+    memcpy( p, data, len );
+    return p + len;
